/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A sample probe dips into the tube, senses liquid level and aspirates 2–20 µL of serum.
   - Two pumps move liquids.
   - The mixture enters a cuvette in a wheel held at 37 °C for a fixed incubation time.
   - Continuous-flow or air-segmented analysers push sample and reagent as one stream broken by air bubbles.
   - Discrete analysers give each test its own cuvette.
   - Throughput is quoted in tests per hour. 600 tests per hour means one result every 3600 s ÷ 600 = 6 s on average.
   - A reagent starts the reaction and absorbance changes.
   - c(sample) is the unknown concentration in mmol/L; c(std) is the calibrator value in mmol/L; A(sample) and A(std) are net absorbances, meaning measured absorbance minus reagent blank and cuvette blank: A = A(measured) − A(blank).
   - In a kinetic assay the analyser takes several readings while the reaction runs at a steady rate and computes ΔA/min, the change in absorbance per minute.
   - ΔA/min is the size of the absorbance change per minute; Vt is the total volume in the cuvette and Vs the sample volume, in the same units; ε is in L mol⁻¹ cm⁻¹; l is the path in cm; 106 turns mol into µmol.
*/
