/* book exam traps: Using single-point calibration outside the linear range: the curve flattens, and a straight-line ratio under-reads the true concentration. | Reading a kinetic assay as an end point: absorbance is still changing; enzyme tests need ΔA/min. | Forgetting carryover: a trace of a very high sample left on the probe raises the next result. A reagent blank does not correct it; thorough washing does, and it is measured with a high-then-low run (worked example 3). | Mixing results from analysers with different wavelengths or path lengths: the same concentration gives a different absorbance. */
