/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A mixture is placed at the start of a column or sheet.
   - In column methods (gas chromatography and HPLC) a peak is described by its retention time tR, the time from injection to the peak maximum; under fixed conditions each compound has its own tR.
   - both distances are measured from the starting line, in the same units; Rf has no unit and lies between 0 and 1.
   - Gas chromatography (GC) uses a carrier gas and a heated column; a flame ionisation detector burns organic molecules and measures ion current.
   - An electrophoresis tank applies an electric field E (V/m) across a buffer-soaked support.
   - v is drift speed (m/s); μ is mobility (m² V⁻¹ s⁻¹); E is field strength (V/m); q is charge (C); f is the friction factor (N·s/m).
   - The sign of the charge follows buffer pH relative to the protein's isoelectric point pI.
   - Paper and cellulose acetate carry serum proteins and haemoglobin variants.
   - Serum protein electrophoresis on cellulose acetate at pH 8.6 gives albumin, α₁, α₂, β and γ bands.
*/
