/* book exam traps: At pH 8.6 serum proteins are net negative and move to the anode; albumin is fastest, γ slowest. | Retention time belongs to column chromatography; Rf belongs to plates and paper; migration distance or mobility belongs to electrophoresis. | SDS-PAGE reports molecular weight, not net charge, because SDS masks the protein's own charge. | In HPLC the small packed particles are the stationary phase; high pressure is needed for flow, not heating. | In isoelectric focusing the protein stops at pH = pI because net charge is zero, even though the field remains on. */
